  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nBertha has been downgraded to a Tropical Depression last evening and \ncontinues to weaken as it tracks westward across southeast Texas, \nwith the system expected to dissipate on Friday. Across South Texas, \nlingering low-level moisture associated with the remnant circulation \nwill support a few showers over the Rio Grande Plains through Friday \nmorning before precipitation chances come to an end. Otherwise, weak \nsurface high pressure and modest mid-level ridging will gradually \nbecome established across the region, promoting mostly clear to \npartly cloudy skies and a return to a more typical summertime \npattern.\n\nWith rain chances diminishing quickly after Friday morning, dry \nconditions are expected through the remainder of the forecast \nperiod. Temperatures will slowly climb through the weekend and \ncontinue warming into early next week, with heat indices supporting \na moderate HeatRisk for most locations. The HeatRisk gradually \nincreases through the week, with portions of our CWA reaching major \nHeatRisk by early next week. No other hazardous weather is \nanticipated besides the heat.",
